From 0687b5c878c1bfac8adbb0af5af1d3dbb55b275d Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: jjude80 <18168364+jjude80@users.noreply.github.com>
Date: Thu, 8 Oct 2026 00:19:36 +0000
Subject: [PATCH 3/4] fix(access): revoke the previous role's grants on
 demotion

3330eb1 made a role change seed the new role's defaults but never withdrew
the old role's. Because role defaults are materialized as ordinary
principal_permission_grants rows, a demoted member kept them -- including
users:manage_permissions and joins:approve, which operator does not imply.

That is a self-escalation path, not just stale state: the demoted operator
can call PATCH /members/:id/permissions and re-grant themselves exactly what
the demotion removed.

pruneSupersededRoleDefaultGrants withdraws only rows with a null
granted_by_user_id. That column is the provenance signal available today: a
grant a human made explicitly carries that human's id and must survive a
demotion, while a role default has none. The prune and the seed run in the
same transaction as the membership update, so a failure cannot leave the
role changed and the grants behind.

This is the asymmetry flagged in review on NEXA-203: updateMemberAndPermissions
already pruned on a role change (delete-all, re-merged), while updateMember
only added. Both now behave the same way on demotion.

Also widens insertMissingPrincipalGrants and pruneSupersededRoleDefaultGrants
to take Pick<Db, "insert" | "select" | "delete"> rather than Db. Neither opens
a transaction, and a transaction handle satisfies the narrower type, which
removes the `tx as unknown as Db` cast at both call sites instead of
laundering a transaction through a full-client type.

Verified: 30/30 in access-service + access-routes-permissions-upgrade, 94/94
across authorization-service, hidden-floor, validators, invite-join-grants and
invite-accept-existing-member, and tsc --noEmit clean. With only the two
service files reverted to cd10736 and the tests kept, both new demotion
assertions fail.

